= Operating system kernel — Simple =
<onlyinclude>The '''kernel''' is the part of an [[Operating system]] that can perform privileged operations. It helps programs share the processor, use memory and communicate with hardware while keeping many operations behind controlled interfaces.</onlyinclude>

== Why it matters ==
Imagine several applications running at once. The kernel helps decide which one uses the CPU, tracks memory mappings, and handles requests to use devices. A program usually asks for a protected service through a ''system call'' rather than directly controlling the hardware.

== What it does not mean ==
The kernel is not the whole operating system. A desktop, command shell, application and many background services can run outside it. Kernel isolation reduces risks but does not make a computer automatically secure.

== Different designs ==
Some kernels include many device drivers and services in privileged code. Others keep a smaller privileged core and delegate more work to separate processes. Both approaches involve engineering tradeoffs.

